From: Vincenzo Grimaldi <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 00:34:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0042 the image had never built (5726 passed)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

.dockerignore excluded tools/ with no negation. The Dockerfile says

    COPY tools/connector_check.py tools/session_tail.py ./tools/

and Docker filters the build CONTEXT before the Dockerfile runs, so those
files were never sent to the daemon and that COPY fails with "file not
found". THE IMAGE HAS NEVER BUILT. 0038's ECS task definition, its one-off
connector_check task and the entire Phase D drill plan all pointed at an
artefact nobody could produce.

test_dockerfile.py had the right idea aimed at the wrong artefact: it
recomputed the import closure and checked the COPY LIST was complete, and
never asked whether the context could deliver it. fly_stack.context_conflicts
reimplements Docker's filter and the suite runs it now.

The fix is NOT `tools/*` + `!tools/connector_check.py`. Re-inclusion under an
excluded directory is a corner of Docker's matcher with a history of not
behaving as read, there is no daemon in this sandbox, and a control whose
behaviour cannot be tested is not a control. So tools/ is simply not
excluded, and the protection it stood for is enforced statically instead:
the Dockerfile names every tools file one by one and a test refuses any COPY
of a bare directory. Same guarantee, asserted rather than hoped for.

MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. Reconstructed the image filesystem from the COPY list
with the .dockerignore filter applied and ran it:

  - the import closure is correct; main.py starts and attaches the carry book
  - it then exits 1, fail-closed, because it cannot reach the venue (D22)
  - session_tail runs from inside the tree and prints all four invariants,
    including "promotion gate file is missing; REFUSING"

THE RUNTIME. 0038 wrote 27 CloudFormation resources for a process that makes
three decisions a day and needs one outbound TLS connection. What this book
needs from a host is five things, and Fly does them in forty lines:

  one process never two      strategy=immediate, one region, one volume
  a disk that survives       [[mounts]] — the ledger is the only thing that
                             knows a position exists (D3)
  a fixed outbound IP        fly ips allocate-egress, so the venue key pins
  secrets at runtime         fly secrets set; the predecessor image shipped
                             live keys in a layer
  it comes back when it dies restart.policy=on-failure, because of D22

THE FLY-SPECIFIC HAZARD. Fly's defaults are built for keeping web apps up.
`rolling` is the DEFAULT strategy and bluegreen/canary boot a new machine
ALONGSIDE the old one — here that is two CarryEngines, two cold starts and a
second $100 short against inventory that backs one, each with a ledger
saying everything is fine. auto_stop_machines is the same hazard from the
other end: it would stop a book holding a live hedge. check() refuses both,
and refuses [http_service]/[[services]] outright — this process serves
nothing and has no business on the public internet.

check() also refuses: a secret-shaped key in [env]; PAPER_TRADING=0,
USE_TESTNET=0 or LIVE_TRADING_ACK in a committed file (a git push may not
arm live trading); BOOK_MODE=carry without CARRY_BORROW_APR or
CARRY_EXECUTION_MODE (build_bot raises, which on Fly is a crash loop); a
health grace_period under the 120s cold start needs; under 512mb. A test
runs config.load() on the shipped [env] so it is verified against the real
parser, and the committed fly.toml is parsed and re-checked every suite.

Still not done, and not doable from here: testnet API keys pinned to the
egress IPv4, and testnet BTC in the wallet — without inventory an overlay
refuses with NO_SPOT_INVENTORY and the drill measures nothing. Until those
exist F2, F3, F4, F7, F8, F10 and F11 stay open and no further code closes
any of them.

